# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# .gitignore — tells git which files to NEVER commit.
# WHY: secrets (.env), generated artifacts (__pycache__), and local-only files
# (the virtualenv, the sqlite db) should not live in version control. Committing
# a virtualenv bloats the repo and breaks on other machines; committing .env
# leaks credentials.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Python bytecode caches — regenerated automatically, never useful in git.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class

# Virtual environment — machine-specific, huge, recreated from requirements.txt.
.venv/
venv/
env/

# Environment variables — SECRETS LIVE HERE. Never commit. Commit .env.example instead.
.env

# Local SQLite database files (we use sqlite for zero-setup local dev).
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3

# Test / coverage artifacts.
.pytest_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/
coverage.xml

# IDE / editor noise.
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp

# OS noise.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# mypy / ruff caches
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Local tooling config — machine-specific, of no use to anyone cloning this.
.claude/

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# KUBERNETES
#
# WHY THIS SECTION EXISTS:
#   The k8s/ directory contains manifests, and one of them exists purely to hold
#   credentials. A Kubernetes Secret manifest is NOT encrypted — the values are
#   base64-ENCODED, which is reversible with a single command:
#
#       echo 'c3VwZXItc2VjcmV0' | base64 -d      ->  super-secret
#
#   So committing a Secret manifest is exactly as bad as committing a plaintext
#   password. Worse, in fact: base64 looks scrambled enough that people assume
#   it's safe, and it survives in git history forever after the "fix".
#
# THE RULE:
#   Commit a *.example.yaml template with placeholder values (so anyone cloning
#   knows which keys exist — same pattern as .env.example above).
#   Never commit the file with the real values in it.
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Any manifest with "secret" in the name is ignored by default...
*secret*.yaml
*secret*.yml

# ...EXCEPT the placeholder templates, which are safe and belong in git.
# NOTE: this negation MUST come after the rules above — git applies the LAST
# matching pattern, so reversing these two blocks would silently ignore the
# templates too.
!*secret*.example.yaml
!*secret*.example.yml

# Kubeconfig files: these hold cluster API endpoints and client certificates,
# i.e. everything needed to authenticate as you against a cluster.
kubeconfig
*.kubeconfig
.kube/

# TLS keys and certificates. The private key is the whole ballgame.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.p12
*.pfx

# Helm dependency downloads (vendored charts) — recreate with `helm dep update`.
k8s/**/charts/
*.tgz
